{"title":"Trust, Then Verify","markdown":{"headingText":"Trust, Then Verify","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-trust-then-verify","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> \"It sounds right\" is the most expensive sentence in AI-assisted work.\n\n**Lab companion:** [Lab 7, Trust, Then Verify](https://michael-borck.github.io/programming-labs/lab-07-trust-then-verify/) — this chapter is the reasoning; the lab is the doing.\n\n## The Concept First\n\nThe agent's explanations are fluent, structured, and confident. That fluency is the product working as designed — and it is also the attack surface. An answer can be well-organised, appropriately detailed, and entirely wrong, in the same paragraph.\n\nSo the rule: **trust is fine; trust is not verification.** You may absolutely accept the agent's account of what it did — provisionally, as a hypothesis. Verification is the separate act of making reality confirm it. The professional workflow is literally the chapter title: trust first, because trusting is cheap; verify always, because confidence is not evidence.\n\n## Understanding Through the Wikipedia Footnote\n\nA Wikipedia article reads confidently and is mostly right. Nobody serious disputes it *because* it is confident — they click the footnote. The footnote is the difference between \"I read a claim\" and \"I checked a source.\"\n\nThe agent's explanations need footnotes, and in software the footnote is always the same thing: **run it**. Not \"look at the code and see if it seems right\" — run the thing on the inputs that matter and observe what comes out. Chapter 5 built the checks; this chapter is about the habit of demanding they actually ran, on evidence you can see.\n\n## Discovering Verification with Your AI Partner\n\n### Exploration 1: Demand the Receipts\n\nAfter any build, the weakest response is \"looks good.\" The strong pattern is to request evidence in a fixed format:\n\n```text\nFor every item in the acceptance checklist, show me:\n1. The exact command or action you performed\n2. The exact output observed\n3. PASS or FAIL against the checklist item\n\nDo not summarise. Do not mark anything PASS that you did not\nactually run in this conversation.\n```\n\n### Exploration 2: The Spot-Check\n\nThen verify one at random — genuinely, yourself, in a terminal or browser. You are not checking whether the agent can do the work; you are calibrating its reliability. If the spot-checked item fails while the receipts claim PASS, you have learned something about this agent, this task type, or this session that no policy could have taught you.\n\n::: {.callout-important title=\"The explanation is not the evidence\"}\nAn agent can explain a bug fix fluently and incorrectly. The explanation is generated the same way the code is — predicted from patterns, one plausible word at a time. Fluency and accuracy are different axes. The only footnote that counts is the observed output of a run you witnessed.\n:::\n\n### Exploration 3: The VET Reflex\n\n*Conversation, Not Delegation* compresses the verification habit into three letters. Apply all three to anything the department produces:\n\n- **V — Verify**: run it on a known input; compare to the expected output from the spec\n- **E — Explain**: can you say *how* it works, in your own words, without reading the code first? If not, you do not own it yet\n- **T — Test**: poke an edge case the agent did not mention. Change one thing. Does it fail loudly or silently?\n\nThe reflex takes minutes. Skipping it is how a plausible function becomes a production incident with your name on the approval.\n\n## From Concept to Code\n\nThe verification habit leaves a record, like everything else in this book. Chapter 3's acceptance file gains a column — observed, with date — and the spec's hostile list becomes the source of edge cases to poke by hand:\n\n```markdown\n| Check | Expected | Observed (2026-09-16) | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Empty submit shows error, sends nothing | error, no data | confirmed in browser | PASS |\n| 200-char name does not break layout | wraps or truncates | NOT tested | ? |\n```\n\nThe `NOT tested` row is the most valuable line in the file. It is an honest list of what you do not know — which is exactly what the next work session should start with.\n\n## Business Connection\n\nVerification is the product you sell when you sell \"AI-era services\". A business does not pay you to prompt; it pays you to be the person whose sign-off means the strange orders were tested. The receipts file — commands, outputs, dates — is how that sign-off survives being questioned in a meeting six weeks later.\n\nThe same file is your defence against the agent's own confidence. When a stakeholder asks \"but the AI said it worked,\" the answer is: \"and I verified it, here is the run.\" That sentence is the entire professional identity this book is building.\n\n## Practice Exercises\n\n**Exercise 8.1 (Level 1).** Demand receipts for your next build in the exact three-part format. Spot-check one at random.\n\n**Exercise 8.2 (Level 2).** Apply the VET reflex to something you accepted yesterday. Run it, explain it, poke it. Record what changed in your assessment.\n\n**Exercise 8.3 (Level 3).** Ask the agent for an explanation of *how* its fix works, then ask it to prove the explanation with a demonstration. Compare.\n\n**Exercise 8.4 (Level 4).** Find a case where the agent's explanation was fluent and wrong. File it in your journal with what the verification caught.\n\n**Exercise 8.5 (Level 5).** Write the verification section of your AGENTS.md: what evidence is required before any change is accepted, for your projects, permanently.\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n- Trust the fluency; verify the claim; the two are different axes\n- Receipts: exact command, exact output, PASS/FAIL — no summaries\n- Spot-check one claimed pass at random, every time\n- The untested list is a to-do list, not a footnote\n\n## Reflection\n\nBefore moving on, ensure you:\n\n- [ ] Collected receipts in the three-part format at least once\n- [ ] Spot-checked one claimed PASS yourself\n- [ ] Applied V — Verify, E — Explain, T — Test — to one deliverable\n- [ ] Recorded at least one NOT tested row honestly\n\n## Your Learning Journal\n\n1. What did your random spot-check find? Was the agent over-, under-, or exactly confident?\n2. Which VET step do you naturally skip — and what would it cost to keep?\n3. What would your receipts file need to contain for a stranger to sign off your work?\n\n## Next Steps\n\nYou can now verify anything the department builds. The next two chapters turn to what the department cannot build: taste, and the long tail of polish — judgement as a deliverable, and the last 20% that makes software feel finished.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"